so just explain what this amendment is  all about and do you think you've got  the numbers to win  it's all about mps having a vote on the  government's policy before it comes into  force and takes away people's civil  liberties  so members of parliament are feeling  increasingly helpless as their  constituents complain about real impacts  on their lives their jobs their  prosperity  indeed their health which come from the  side effects of covet measures  so we're not proposing to take the power  from ministers to decide what's done or  take away the power of the business of  the house of commons none of those worst  fears we're just saying  that the government should use the  procedure that puts a statutory  instrument before the house  for a debate and the decision in a sense  i'm saying mps  should be sharing in the dreadful burden  of decision in these circumstances  and not just retrospectively being asked  to approve what the government's done  and to ask answer your question  there are plenty of mps who would vote  uh for this amendment if it is selected  and we think it will be selected  it's really down to labor the snp and  the liberal democrats  if it comes to it i have to say we don't  really want a rebellion  we're trying to support the government  in getting this done by saying  let us vote on these measures and  support the government let's have policy  which enjoys our consent  so if the opposition parties do back the  amendment labor the  dems etc do you think you've got the  numbers to get it through i'm  well i'm certain at the moment but that  that's really as i say we would prefer  to avoid  this coming to a division i'm afraid  it's in the nature of the way parliament  works that things happen like this  but i'm i back boris johnson i want him  to succeed  but we do need to share with the  government the burden of decision on  these measures and not just  come in you know days or weeks later  retrospectively perhaps voting on  measures which have subsequently been  amended it's been a nonsense you know  you look at the public health act 1984  measures came in by the time we voted on  them they'd been changed once or twice  and we had charles walker former head of  the procedure committee he wouldn't mind  me saying  you know stood up in the house of  commons and wasn't sure which measure he  was voting on well if somebody who's  chaired the procedure committee  can't be sure well then we're into a  nonsense position where  mp's democratic consent for really  important draconian policy  has ended up being missing so we're  trying to put this right and actually  it's a very modest proposal  that mps should vote on law before it  comes into effect and takes away  people's liberties  you say it's a modest proposal you say  you're trying to share the burden but at  the same time if there's anything that  the last two six months or so has told  us  it's how fast this virus can move is  there not a risk  that your amendment effectively slows  the government down  it stops it from taking that very quick  action which might be necessary in the  fight against covert 19.  well we need a solution that does allow  for that fast and effective action which  also rules out any possibility of  mischief from the labour party but which  also gives mps  ordinarily the opportunity to vote  before things come into action but i  think if you if everybody reflects on  what  has happened the prime minister's made  announcements about what's to come into  a  in into effect days later there really  has been time now  for measures to be properly debated i  think at the time we put the coronavirus  act through  you could see that it was an emergency  in the reasonable worst case of the time  we needed to just get that act through  and it was an  awful thing to face but now we're much  further along  now treatments have improved now there's  more testing there  is time to have a debate on a statutory  instrument we're only really talking  about a two hour debate  on a statutory instrument with no  amendments to that instrument that's the  way it works  vote on it if if parliament votes for it  then the minister can sign it into law  and this is the normal procedure  and i think we need to reach an  agreement with the government whereby if  it was a genuine emergency they could  act on  what's called the maid affirmative  procedure but i'm afraid this is very  procedural but  there is a really is a scope here to  solve the problem we've done it before  with the eu withdrawal act we can do it  now  um i'm interested to get your thoughts a  bit more broadly  on some of the measures and the  direction that the government is going  in i noticed you used the word draconian  in the interview a bit earlier you  previously said uh it's time for us to  actually start living like a free people  not subjecting ourselves to constantly  shifting  legal requirements what exactly do you  mean what are your concerns  well a hundred acts of parliament have  been used to put in place i think it's  242 statutory instruments  the telegraph has reported i think it  was yesterday that there have been 200  changes well when you've got a body of  law  that large changing that fast i doubt  really anyone understands what that law  is  much beyond the people who wrote the  frequently asked questions available on  the gov  uk website which now run to 22 pages  when you print them  so we're in an environment where you  really can't know whether you're a  criminal or not  with this much law coming into force and  changing so fast  and that's why i've said this is not a  fit environment for free people  you know being so you don't feel that  we're being treated like a free people  at the minute  that's what i'm saying absolutely yes i  mean how do people think that liberty  dies it dies like this with with  government exercising draconian powers  without parliamentary scrutiny in  advance undermining the rule of law by  having a shifting blanket of rules that  no one can understand  i mean it's extremely serious i don't  think i look like a hysterical person to  you  i'm saying that this is a very serious  moment rishi sunak brought up this issue  that we need to learn to live without  fear at the moment moment the public are  being deliberately told  measure told that they should be afraid  the idea i think the phrase used was  that the sense of personal risk needed  to be raised well the government's been  frightening people into compliance and  it hasn't been working  if you look at figures from king's  college the figures for  how the proportion of people isolating  if they've got covid  symptoms are very low i think it's 18  you know this  this isn't working trying to frighten  people and actually what we need to do  is all grow up  and try and live a bit more virtuously  have a bit more faith hope courage  temperance prudence all those great  virtues which actually make a society  one worth having  so that's why i want government to come  to parliament  give mps a vote before things come into  effect and let us share in the burden of  decision and represent our constituents  which is what we're elected to do at the  same time though  infection numbers are going one way at  the minute and  all the evidence suggests that the  government has actually got the public's  backing on a lot of the restrictions  that it's put in place according to a  yougov survey this week  78 of people support the government's  new measures so are you actually on the  wrong side of the argument  here well no but this is the thing about  surveys  versus what people actually do and i  know what the surveys say and i also  know  what the king's college evidence says  about compliance i'm afraid there is a  disconnect between people's intentions  and what they actually do and that  that's really what i'm  pointing to i'm well aware of the case  numbers going up and i'm well aware that  this is a  dangerous disease for people with  comorbidities but i'm also well aware  that for people under the age of 60  without other pre-existing conditions it  is not an especially dangerous disease  and this is one of the cruelties of it  so i'm very interested in listening to  your debate between scientists  scientists are only human too there is a  range of interpretations available on  the data that we have i think it's very  important that we're really  practical and realistic about science in  an  at this kind of environment of  uncertainty and we have to balance the  cost of lockdown the cost of these  measures  against the cost of the disease and the  department of health's own figures show  that lockdown's been more expensive than  the disease  and very quickly because we are running  out of time do you think that boris  johnson should listen a bit more to his  chancellor then  well i want the two of them to succeed  as a team i've got great admiration  for them both in a sense what we need is  boris back with his bounce with his mojo  he's got a great chancellor in rishi and  i support them both  okay uh and just finally there were  reports this morning that paul  daycare the former editor of the daily  mail  critic of the bbc is boris johnson's top  choice  for the job of ofcom chairman is that an  appointment you'd welcome  of course it is yes i'd be delighted and  of course i'd like to see charles moore  go in as chairman of the bbc i think  this would be great news and i think  anyone suggesting we  we were somehow appointing our preferred  people needs to look at the record of  others who've been appointed to these  roles  and try to be a bit again more realistic  about politics and policy and who should  be  appointed by governments why would it be  great news then  well because they're conservatives and  they would they might actually start to  look at the way  the media functions and ensure there's  some impartiality i can  i can assure you that from my point of  view time and again during interviews  i felt that there was a consensus  thought on the side of the broadcaster  which was not on the side of moderate  mainstream conservatism so  i look forward to them perhaps being  appointed and being a bit more  conservative and pragmatic in what is  reported
 

